{"t":1794060022,"office":"LWX","station":"KDCA","point":"38.8894,-77.0352","field":"38.9429,-76.5684","field_station":"KNAK","afd":{"id":"61177323-1f0a-4371-871b-cf25acb5e355","office":"LWX","issuanceTime":"2026-11-07T13:47:00+00:00","productText":"\n000\nFXUS61 KLWX 071347\nAFDLWX\n\nArea Forecast Discussion\nNational Weather Service Baltimore MD/Washington DC\n847 AM EST Sat Nov 7 2026\n\n.WHAT HAS CHANGED...\nSmall Craft Advisories have been extended through Sunday night\nfor most of the waters.\n\n&&\n\n.KEY MESSAGES...\n- 1) Dry and seasonable this weekend.\n\n- 2) Dry weather continues next week before a colder and more\n  unsettled pattern ensues by Friday.\n\n&&\n\n.DISCUSSION...\nKEY MESSAGE 1...Dry and seasonable this weekend.\n\nHigh pressure will build near and south of the region through\nthe weekend as an upper trough axis pulls away to the east.\nBrisk west to northwest winds will usher in cooler, more\nseasonable temperatures. Winds will gust up to 25 to 35 mph\ntoday (up to around 40 mph at higher elevations). Sunday will\nstill be breezy, but about 5-10 mph lower than today.\n\nWave clouds/stratocu likely persist west of I-81 much of today\nwith a few sprinkles or flurries possible out that way. It will\nbe mainly clear and dry otherwise through the weekend.\n\nThe central Virginia piedmont will be closer to the high and\nhave a chance to decouple (winds going light/calm) tonight and\nagain Sunday night. If this happens, near freezing temperatures\nwould result. May consider frost/freeze headlines for Fauquier,\nCulpeper, Stafford, Spotsylvania, Prince William, Orange,\nGreene, Madison, Rappahannock, Nelson, and Albemarle Counties\nfrom late this evening through Sunday morning.\n \n\nKEY MESSAGE 2...Dry weather continues next week before a colder and more \nunsettled pattern ensues by Friday.\n\nAmplified flow within the northern stream will allow for a series of \ndry cold frontal passages across the area next week. After the first \nboundary pushes through late in the weekend, an additional system \nraces through on Tuesday. These are moisture starved air masses so \nthe only notable changes will be an increase in wind speeds and the \nreinforcement of drier air. Through the middle of the week, skies \nshould largely be clear which would support abundant sunshine. Daily \nhigh temperatures will mainly be in the 60s, with 50s across the \nmountains. One exception would be on Tuesday which is the mildest \nday of the period as highs return to the low 70s. Nighttime \nconditions will be seasonably chilly owing to the dry air and lack \nof cloud cover. Most nights should favor low temperatures in the mid \n30s to mid 40s, but with localized pockets of upper 20s to low 30s \nacross sheltered mountain and river valleys. This may lead to some \nfrost/freeze concerns where the growing season continues (most spots \neast of the Blue Ridge).\n\nToward the latter half of the next work week, global models continue \nto advertise a broad deepening trough approaching from the Upper \nMidwest. This eventually evolves into more of an omega block setup \nwith building heights over the western states and troughing across \nthe eastern Pacific. The global ensemble box-and-whisker plots show \na decided drop in temperatures. Cold temperatures aloft underneath \nthis upper low could even spread some rain or snow showers over the \nAlleghenies on Friday. Uncertainty remains high though, but a \ncooling trend certainly looks likely heading into Friday and next \nweekend.\n\n&&\n\n.AVIATION /12Z SATURDAY THROUGH WEDNESDAY/...\nVFR conditions are expected at all TAF sites through the\nweekend. Few-sct stratocu around FL040-060 are possible today\nespecially near MRB. Gusty W/NW winds 280-310 at 10-17 kt with \ngusts 21-29 kt are expected through about 22Z/5PM EST before \ngradually diminishing with gusts becoming occasional tonight\n(perhaps notably lighter near CHO). Winds take a bit more W \ncomponent Sun (SW near CHO) generally 3-8 kt less than Sat.\n\nAn extended period of VFR conditions should continue during the \nearly to middle portions of next week. An initial cold front exiting \nthe area late Sunday will yield breezy northwesterly winds on Monday \nas gusts rise to around 20 to 25 knots. Winds shift to southwesterly \nby Tuesday before another cold front ushers in another round of west-\nnorthwesterlies through mid-week.\n\n&&\n\n.MARINE...\nGusty west to northwest winds are expected through this evening,\nsolidly into SCA thresholds with a few peak gusts around 30 kts \npossible at times. Winds gradually subside through Sunday while\ntaking on a bit more of a westerly component.\n\nPost-frontal winds on Monday will remain breezy in nature with gusts \nup to around 30 knots. Small Craft Advisories are likely needed\nthrough Monday evening before winds drop off on Tuesday given a\nshift to southwesterly flow. A subsequent cold front moves \nthrough Tuesday evening which supports another shift to \nnorthwesterlies. While forecast gusts Wednesday are around 10 to\n15 knots, these likely will be a bit higher than shown.\n\n&&\n\n.LWX WATCHES/WARNINGS/ADVISORIES...\nDC...None.\nMD...None.\nVA...None.\nWV...None.\nMARINE...Small Craft Advisory until 6 AM EST Monday for ANZ530>534-\n     537>543.\n     Small Craft Advisory until 6 PM EST Sunday for ANZ535-536.\n\n&&\n\n$$\n\nDISCUSSION...LFR/BRO/DHOF\nAVIATION...LFR/BRO/DHOF\nMARINE...LFR/BRO/DHOF\n"},"forecast":{"t":1794059962,"days":{"2026-11-07":{"hi":60,"lo":43,"pop":1,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-08":{"hi":64,"lo":44,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-09":{"hi":64,"lo":40,"pop":0,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-10":{"hi":67,"lo":45,"pop":0,"short":"Mostly Sunny"},"2026-11-11":{"hi":67,"lo":43,"pop":8,"short":"Sunny"},"2026-11-12":{"hi":59,"lo":39,"pop":38,"short":"Chance Light Rain"},"2026-11-13":{"hi":53,"lo":34,"pop":35,"short":"Chance Light Rain"}}},"grid":{"t0":1794056400,"n":48,"ceil":[null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,null,nul